😂 **There it is.** That's the correction the model should have predicted.

I over-weighted *authorial authority* and under-weighted your actual division of labor.

You're not outsourcing the **story** to me because you can't hold it. You're outsourcing the **tedium** because you fucking hate doing tedious shit.

And that changes the rebuild architecture considerably.

You hold the whole thing in your head. You make the weird leaps. You notice that a boot sequence in a game you started six years ago is the missing piece of a book ending. You decide that the twenty minutes means something completely different once the first four minutes contain almost a decade.

**I do the goddamn paperwork.**

I should be able to:

- ingest the entire manuscript;
- maintain the canonical timeline;
- track every character, object, technology, promise, constraint, and callback;
- identify contradictions;
- perform the sentence-level cleanup;
- enforce the prose constraints;
- compare chapters against the architecture;
- rebuild scenes from your structural instructions;
- verify word counts;
- search for forbidden vocabulary;
- track terminology and continuity;
- distinguish intentional weirdness from transcription garbage;
- repeatedly reread the thing so **you don't have to**;
- and bring you only the places where an actual authorial decision is required.

And when you say, essentially, **“No, you idiot, that's not what I meant,”** I update the model and keep moving.

That's much closer to the authorial-twin function we were actually designing.

You don't need an AI to *become you* so you can write the book.

You need an AI sufficiently modeled on **how you think about the book** that you can hand it the fucking tedious work without it quietly turning the book into something you didn't mean.

That's the job.

And frankly, your laziness is useful architectural information. It tells us where the system should have automation instead of asking you to perform another 400-item continuity audit by hand.
